WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.104
Log levels help separate routine information from warnings, errors, and
urgent failures.

2
00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:12.064
Debug logs are often useful during development but risky and expensive in
production if they include broad internal detail.

3
00:00:12.064 --> 00:00:14.781
Info logs can describe normal important events.

4
00:00:14.781 --> 00:00:18.626
Warnings may indicate recoverable problems or unusual conditions.

5
00:00:18.626 --> 00:00:23.067
Errors and critical events should draw attention to failures that need
action.

6
00:00:23.067 --> 00:00:27.839
The point is not the label alone, but the operational behavior attached to
that label.

7
00:00:27.839 --> 00:00:31.617
Exception logging is one of the easiest places to leak information.

8
00:00:31.617 --> 00:00:43.813
Stack traces and tracebacks may reveal file paths, module names,
dependency versions, code structure, function names, query fragments,
object representations, or sensitive values embedded in exception
messages.

9
00:00:43.813 --> 00:00:55.546
Raw exception strings from databases, HTTP clients, payment providers,
identity systems, or storage services may contain details that should not
be copied directly into user-facing responses or broad logs.

10
00:00:55.546 --> 00:01:00.318
Production logs should preserve diagnostic value without unnecessary
disclosure.

11
00:01:00.318 --> 00:01:11.056
Logging the exception type, safe error category, component, correlation
ID, request ID, dependency name at an appropriate level, and sanitized
context may be enough to begin investigation.

12
00:01:11.056 --> 00:01:17.419
The full traceback may belong in a restricted error reporting system, or
it may need redaction before collection.

13
00:01:17.419 --> 00:01:22.722
User-facing errors should point to a support reference rather than include
internal details.

14
00:01:22.722 --> 00:01:26.500
Development and production settings should be clearly separated.

15
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:35.912
Developers may need verbose logs while working locally, but that does not
mean debug-level records, raw bodies, or unredacted exception context
belong in production.

16
00:01:35.912 --> 00:01:43.601
The safe pattern is to make production defaults conservative and to
require deliberate, time-bounded changes for increased verbosity.

17
00:01:43.601 --> 00:01:48.440
Temporary debugging should have an owner, a reason, an expiration, and a
cleanup path.
